<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">

{%- comment -%}
  Title and description are each used three times below, so resolve them once.
  A post that sets `description` in front matter gets it everywhere - page meta,
  OpenGraph, and the feed. Without one, everything falls back to the site
  description rather than to a machine-made excerpt.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign pageTitle = title | default: metadata.title -%}
{%- assign pageDescription = description | default: metadata.description -%}
{%- assign pagePath = page.url | url -%}
{%- assign pageUrl = metadata.url | append: pagePath -%}
{%- assign isPost = false -%}
{%- if tags contains "post" -%}{%- assign isPost = true -%}{%- endif -%}

<title>{% if title %}{{ title | escape }} · {{ metadata.title }}{% else %}{{ metadata.title }}{% endif %}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ pageDescription | escape }}">

<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ '/css/main.css' | url }}">
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ pageUrl }}">

<link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="{{ metadata.title }}" href="{{ metadata.url }}{{ '/feed.xml' | url }}">

{%- comment -%}
  LinkedIn, Discord, and Slack all build their link previews from these. Without
  them a pasted link renders as a bare URL. Absolute URLs throughout - a preview
  scraper has no page to resolve a relative one against.
{%- endcomment %}
<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ metadata.title }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ pageTitle | escape }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ pageDescription | escape }}">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ pageUrl }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="{% if isPost %}article{% else %}website{% endif %}">
<meta property="og:image" content="{{ metadata.url }}{{ '/img/og-card-v2.png' | url }}">
<meta property="og:image:width" content="2400">
<meta property="og:image:height" content="1260">
<meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ metadata.title }} — {{ metadata.subtitle }}">
{%- if isPost %}
<meta property="article:published_time" content="{{ page.date | date: '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ' }}">
<meta property="article:author" content="{{ metadata.author.name }}">
{%- endif %}

{%- comment -%}
  Despite the name, Discord and several other scrapers read the twitter:* tags
  too. The rest they take from the og:* tags above.
{%- endcomment %}
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
